111\nL1810:     0011100000_0111100000_0011111100_1111111111_0111111101\nL1811:     `\nL1812: 【38†Improve this answer】\nL1813: \nL1814: Follow [Input]\nL1815: \nL1816: 【39†edited Jul 19, 2018 at 7:54】\nL1817: \nL1818: answered Jul 5, 2018 at 8:42\nL1819: \nL1820: 【31†Peter Taylor】\nL1821: \nL1822: 43.6k 4 4 gold badges 74 74 silver badges 195 195 bronze badges\nL1823: \nL1824:   * 2\nL1825: \nL1826: Finally managed to get a search for <43 with n=12 to complete without exceeding the memory which can be allocated to a single process; it took 7.5 days and didn't find any improvement, so unless I broke the SAT solver the result for n=12 is also optimal under the bbox assumption.\nL1827: \nL1828: –  【31†Peter Taylor】\nL1829: Commented Jul 19, 2018 at 7:30\nL1830: \nL1831:   * 1\nL1832: \nL1833: Tonnes of effort right here! Very impressive.\nL1834: \nL1835: –  【40†Jonathan Allan】\nL1836: \nL1837: Commented Jul 29, 2018 at 13:51\nL1838: \nL1839:   * Can we add these results to the OEIS? Or they cannot be considered optimal as they require the bounding box assumption?\nL1840: \nL1841: –  【41†Dmitry Kamenetsky】\nL1842: \nL1843: Commented May 15, 2022 at 9:58\nL1844: \nL1845: 【34†Add a comment】  |\nL1846: \nL1847: [Input]\nL1848: \nL1849: 9\nL1850: \nL1851: [Input]\nL1852: \nL1853: In the interest of getting the process started, here's a quick (but not very optimal) answer.\nL1854: ### Pattern:\nL1855: \nL1856:     `n = 8:\nL1857:     ########\nL1858:      ######\nL1859:       #####\nL1860:        ####\nL1861:         ###\nL1862:          ##\nL1863:     `\nL1864: \nL1865: Take a triangle with length n - 1, stick an extra square onto the corner, and cut off the bottom square.\nL1866: ### Proof that all n-ominos fit:\nL1867: \nL1868: Note that any n-omino can fit in a rectangle with length + width at most n + 1.\nL1869: \nL1870: If an n-omino fits in a rectangle with length + width at most n, it fits nicely in the triangle (which is the union of all such rectangles). If it happens to use the cut-off square, transposing it will fit in the triangle.\nL1871: Otherwise, we have a chain with at most one branch. We can always fit one end of the chain into the extra square (prove this with casework), and the rest fits into a rectangle with length + width at most n, reducing to the case above.\nL1872: \nL1873: The only case where the above doesn't work is the case where we use both the extra square and the cut-off square. There's only one such n-omino (the long L), and that one fits inside the triangle transposed.\nL1874: ### Code (Python 2):\nL1875: \nL1876:     `def f(n):\nL1877:         if n < 7:\nL1878:             return [0, 1, 2, 4, 6, 9, 12][n]\nL1879:         return n * (n - 1) / 2\nL1880:     `\nL1881: ### Table:\nL1882: \nL1883:     ` 1:   1\nL1884:      2:   2\nL1885:      3:   4\nL1886:      4:   6\nL1887:      5:   9\nL1888:      6:  12\nL1889:      7:  21\nL1890:      8:  28\nL1891:      9:  36\nL1892:     10:  45\nL1893:     11:  55\nL1894:     12:  66\nL1895:     13:  78\nL1896:     14:  91\nL1897:     15: 105\nL1898:     16: 120\nL1899:     17: 136\nL1900:     18: 153\nL1901:     19: 171\nL1902:     20: 190\nL1903:     ... more cases can be generated if necessary.\nL1904:     `\nL1905: \nL1906: 【42†Improve this answer】\nL1907: \nL1908: Follow [Input]\nL1909: \nL1910: 【43†edited Jun 26, 2018 at 20:52】\nL1911: \nL1912: answered Jun 26, 2018 at 18:55\nL1913: \nL1914: user48543\nL1915:   * I think you can remove the bottom two squares for sufficiently large \\$n\\$. Mock proof: Aside from the two squares on the ends of the top row the shape has a mirror symmetry. Thus any shape that occupies the one of the bottom two either can be mirrored out of them or occupies the top right square. All the ones that occupy one of the bottom two and the top right, are within a 3x(n-1) footprint. We have a 3x(n-1) rectangle in the top 3 rows minus a single corner.\nL1916: The remaining shapes can only occupy a max of 2 corners in the bounding box, thus they can be rotated to fit in the top 3 rows.\nL1917: –  【44†Wheat Wizard】 ♦\nL1918: \nL1919: Commented Jun 26, 2018 at 22:59\nL1920: \nL1921:   * The character limit kind of got me. If you need more explanation I can clarify.\nL1922: \nL1923: –  【44†Wheat Wizard】 ♦\nL1924: \nL1925: Commented Jun 26, 2018 at 23:00\nL1926: \nL1927:   * @CatWizard I see what you mean. I wouldn't be surprised if you could extend it to removing about half the rows (for large n).\nL1928: \nL1929: –  user48543\nL1930: \nL1931: Commented Jun 26, 2018 at 23:29\nL1932: \nL1933: 【34†Add a comment】  |\nL1934: \nL1935: [Input]\nL1936: \nL1937: 8\nL1938: \nL1939: [Input]\nL1940: ## C#, score: 1, 2, 4, 6, 9, 12, 17, 20, 26, 31, 38, 44\nL1941: \nL1942:     `#\nL1943: \nL1944:     ##\nL1945: \nL1946:     #..\nL1947:     ###\nL1948: \nL1949:     .##.\nL1950:     ####\nL1951: \nL1952:     ..#..\nL1953:     #####\nL1954:     ###..\nL1955: \nL1956:     ##....\nL1957:     ######\nL1958:     ####..\nL1959: \nL1960:     ..##...\nL1961:     .###...\nL1962:     #######\nL1963:     .#####.\nL1964: \nL1965:     ..###...\nL1966:     ..###...\nL1967:     ..######\nL1968:     ########\nL1969: \nL1970:     ..##.....\nL1971:     .###.....\nL1972:     #######..\nL1973:     #########\nL1974:     ..#####..\nL1975: \nL1976:     .###......\nL1977:     .####.....\nL1978:     .######...\nL1979:     ##########\nL1980:     .########.\nL1981: \nL1982:     .###.......\nL1983:     .####......\nL1984:     .####......\nL1985:     .#######...\nL1986:     ###########\nL1987:     .#########.\nL1988: \nL1989:     .####.......\nL1990:     #####.......\nL1991:     .#####......\nL1992:     ############\nL1993:     .##########.\nL1994:     .########...\nL1995:     `\nL1996: * * *\nL1997: \nL1998: The output format of the program is a bit more compact.\nL1999: \nL2000: This uses a seeded random approach, and I've optimised the seeds. I enforce a bounding box constraint which is both plausible and consistent with the known data for small values of n. If that constraint is indeed valid then\nL2001:   1. The output is optimal up to n=8 (by brute force validation, not included).\nL2002:   2. The number of optimal solutions (distinct up to symmetry) begins `1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 6, 63, 6`.\nL2003: \nL2004:     `using System;\nL2005:     using System.Collections.Generic;\nL2006:     using System.Diagnostics;\nL2007:     using System.Linq;\nL2008: \nL2009:     namespace Sandbox\nL2010:     {\nL2011:         class FreePolyomino : IEquatable<FreePolyomino>\nL2012:         {\nL2013:             public static void Main()\nL2014:             {\nL2015:                 for (int i = 1; i < 12; i++)\nL2016:                 {\nL2017:                     int seed;\nL2018:                     switch (i) {\nL2019:                         default: seed = 1103199029; break;\nL2020:                         case 9: seed = 693534956; break; // 26\nL2021:                         case 10: seed = 2005746461; break; // 31\nL2022:                         case 11: seed = 377218946; break; // 38\nL2023:                         case 12: seed = 1281379414; break; // 44\nL2024:                     }\nL2025: \nL2026:                     var rnd = new Random(seed);\nL2027:                     var polys = FreePolyomino.All(i);\nL2028:                     var minUnion = FreePolyomino.RandomMinimalUnion2(polys, rnd, i, (i + 1) >> 1);\nL2029:                     Console.WriteLine($\"{i}\\t{minUnion.Weight}\\t{minUnion}\");\nL2030:                 }\nL2031:             }\nL2032: \nL2033:             internal FreePolyomino(OrientedPolyomino orientation)\nL2034:             {\nL2035:                 var orientations = new HashSet<OrientedPolyomino>();\nL2036:                 orientations.Add(orientation);\nL2037:                 var tmp = orientation.Rot90(); orientations.Add(tmp);\nL2038:                 tmp = tmp.Rot90(); orientations.Add(tmp);\nL2039:                 tmp = tmp.Rot90(); orientations.Add(tmp);\nL2040:                 tmp = tmp.FlipV(); orientations.Add(tmp);\nL2041:                 tmp = tmp.Rot90(); orientations.Add(tmp);\nL2042:                 tmp = tmp.Rot90(); orientations.Add(tmp);\nL2043:                 tmp = tmp.Rot90(); orientations.Add(tmp);\nL2044: \nL2045:                 OrientedPolyominos = orientations.OrderBy(x => x).ToArray();\nL2046:             }\nL2047:             public IReadOnlyList<OrientedPolyomino> OrientedPolyominos { get; private set; }\nL2048: \nL2049:             public OrientedPolyomino CanonicalOrientation => OrientedPolyominos[0];\nL2050: \nL2051:             public static IEnumerable<FreePolyomino> All(int numCells)\nL2052:             {\nL2053:                 if (numCells < 1) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(numCells));\nL2054:                 if (numCells == 1) return new FreePolyomino[] { new FreePolyomino(OrientedPolyomino.Unit) };\nL2055:                 // We do this in two phases because identifying two equal oriented polyominos is faster than first building\nL2056:                 // free polyominos and then identifying that they're equal.\nL2057:                 var oriented = new HashSet<OrientedPolyomino>();\nL2058:                 foreach (var smaller in All(numCells - 1))\nL2059:                 {\nL2060:                     // We can add a cell to a side. The easiest way to do this is to add to the bottom of one of the rotations.\nL2061:                     // TODO Optimise by distinguishing the symmetries.\nL2062:                     foreach (var orientation in smaller.OrientedPolyominos)\nL2063:                     {\nL2064:                         int h = orientation.BBHeight;\nL2065:                         var bottomRow = orientation.Rows[h - 1];\nL2066:                         for (int deltax = 0; deltax < orientation.BBWidth; deltax++)\nL2067:                         {\nL2068:                             if (((bottomRow >> deltax) & 1) == 1) oriented.Add(orientation.Union(OrientedPolyomino.Unit, deltax, h));\nL2069:                         }\nL2070:                     }\nL2071: \nL2072:                     // We can add a cell in the middle, provided it connects up.\nL2073:                     var canon = smaller.CanonicalOrientation;\nL2074:                     uint prev = 0, curr = 0, next = canon.Rows[0];\nL2075:                     for (int y = 0; y < canon.BBHeight; y++)\nL2076:                     {\nL2077:                         (prev, curr, next ) = (curr, next, y + 1 < canon.BBHeight ? canon.Rows[y + 1] : 0);\nL2078:                         uint valid = (prev | next | (curr << 1) | (curr >> 1)) & ~curr;\nL2079:                         for (int x = 0; x < canon.BBWidth; x++)\nL2080:                         {\nL2081:                             if (((valid >> x) & 1) == 1) oriented.Add(canon.Union(OrientedPolyomino.Unit, x, y));\nL2082:                         }\nL2083:                     }\nL2084:                 }\nL2085:                 // Now cluster the oriented polyominos into equivalence classes under dihedral symmetry.\nL2086:                 return new HashSet<FreePolyomino>(oriented.Select(orientation => new FreePolyomino(orientation)));\nL2087:             }\nL2088: \nL2089:             internal static OrientedPolyomino RandomMinimalUnion2(IEnumerable<FreePolyomino> polys, Random rnd, int maxWidth, int maxHeight, int target = int.MaxValue)\nL2090:             {\nL2091:                 var union = OrientedPolyomino.Unit;\nL2092:                 foreach (var poly in polys.Shuffle(rnd).ToList())\nL2093:                 {\nL2094:                     union = poly.MinimalUnion(union, rnd, maxWidth, maxHeight);\nL2095:                     if (union.Weight > target) throw new Exception(\"Too heavy\");\nL2096:                 }\nL2097: \nL2098:                 return new FreePolyomino(union).CanonicalOrientation;\nL2099:             }\nL2100: \nL2101:             private OrientedPolyomino MinimalUnion(FreePolyomino other, Random rnd, int maxWidth, int maxHeight)\nL2102:             {\nL2103:                 // Choose the option which does least work.\nL2104:                 return OrientedPolyominos.Count <= other.OrientedPolyominos.Count ?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA395495 - OEIS (https://oeis.org/A395495)\nciteturn5view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view0\",\"lineno\":14}); Total lines: 142\nL0: 【0†login】\nL1: \nL2: The OEIS is supported by 【1†the many generous donors to the OEIS Foundation†oeisf.org】.\nL3: \nL4: 【2†Winners in the A400K Celebration】\nL5: \nL6: [Input]\nL7: \nL8: [Input]\nL9: \nL10: 【3†Hints】\nL11: \nL12: (Greetings from 【4†The On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences】!)\nL13: \nL14: A395495\nL15: \nL16: Minimum number of cells in a connected polyomino on the square grid that contains every fixed n-omino as a translated subregion.\nL17: \nL18: 1\nL19: \nL20: 1, 3, 5, 9, 13, 18, 24, 31, 39\nL21: \nL22: (【5†list】; 【6†graph】; 【7†refs】; 【8†listen】; 【9†history】; 【10†text】; 【11†internal format】)\nL23: \nL24: OFFSET\nL25: 1,2\nL26: \nL27: COMMENTS\nL28: \nL29: Containment is translation-only: the contained piece may not be rotated or reflected. Number of fixed n-ominoes is 【12†A001168】. Each of a(1) through a(9) is proved via SAT + CEGAR connectivity and carries a DRAT UNSAT certificate at k = a(n) - 1 that is independently verified by drat-trim. A pure-Python geometric containment verifier also re-confirms that every reported container does contain every fixed n-omino as a translate.\nL30: Conjecture: a(n) = (n^2 - n + 6)/2 for n >= 4, with a(n) = 2n - 1 for n in {1, 2, 3}. Equivalently a(n) = 【13†A000217】(n-1) + 3 for n >= 4. Predicts a(10) = 48.\nL31: At n = 7 every minimum container has a single 1-cell interior hole. SAT model-enumeration over the 7 X 7 bounding box (the natural search box, which must contain both the horizontal and vertical n-cell I-omino) finds exactly four 24-cell containers; all four are the 25-cell discrete L_1 ball of radius 3 (centered in the 7 X 7 box) minus one cell from the D_4 orbit at L_1 distance 2 from the center, and all contain a hole. A wider 8 X 8 search at k = 23 returns UNSAT (LRAT certified).\nL32: The hole is therefore a structural feature of a(7), distinguishing this sequence geometrically from the purely numerical match 【14†A350660】. For n in {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9} the optimal container is simply connected.\nL33: LINKS\nL34: \nL35: 【15†Table of n, a(n) for n=1..9.】\nL36: \nL37: Peter Exley, 【16†Solver code, data, and figures†github.com】, GitHub.\nL38: \nL39: EXAMPLE\nL40: \nL41: Optimal containers, with one example per illustrated n. Cells are marked '#'; empty cells are '.'.\nL42: \nL43: The 1 container with bounding box 3 X 3:\nL44: \nL45: . # .\nL46: # # #\nL47: \nL48: . # .\nL49: \nL50: .\nL51: \nL52: The 1 container with bounding box 4 X 4:\nL53: \nL54: . . # .\nL55: \nL56: . # # .\nL57: \nL58: # # # #\nL59: \nL60: . # # .\nL61: \nL62: .\nL63: \nL64: The 1 container with bounding box 5 X 5:\nL65: \nL66: . . # . .\nL67: \nL68: . # # # .\nL69: \nL70: # # # # #\nL71: \nL72: . # # # .\nL73: \nL74: . . # . .\nL75: \nL76: .\nL77: \nL78: One of 4 D_4-equivalent containers with bounding box 7 X 7 (the single dot at row 3 column 3 is the interior hole):\nL79: \nL80: . . . # . . .\nL81: \nL82: . . # # # . .\nL83: \nL84: . # . # # # .\nL85: # # # # # # #\nL86: \nL87: . # # # # # .\nL88: \nL89: . . # # # . .\nL90: \nL91: . . . # . . .\nL92: \nL93: .\nL94: \nL95: The 1 container with bounding box 9 X 9:\nL96: \nL97: . . . . . # . . .\nL98: \nL99: . . . . # # . . .\nL100: \nL101: . . . # # # . . .\nL102: \nL103: . . # # # # # # #\nL104: \nL105: . # # # # # # # #\nL106: # # # # # # # # #\nL107: \nL108: . . . # # # . . .\nL109: \nL110: . . . # # # . . .\nL111: \nL112: . . . # # # . . .\nL113: \nL114: CROSSREFS\nL115: \nL116: Cf. 【17†A327094】 (the free-piece analog), 【12†A001168】 (number of fixed n-ominoes (the family containe